\begin{abstract}
Atrial fibrillation (AF) raises stroke risk, and electrocardiogram (ECG) models that predict its onset are explained only after training, by attribution methods that agree poorly. We asked whether a model whose ECG evidence passes through readable factors predicts incident AF from one sinus-rhythm 12-lead ECG more accurately than a clinical model and a convolutional neural network (CNN). We also asked where in the cardiac cycle its evidence lies. We propose the block-term tensor network (BTTN), a Cox survival network on a frequency by time by lead spectrogram tensor. Each block projects the tensor onto a frequency factor and a lead factor, and age, sex and ECG intervals enter a separate linear clinical term that bypasses these factors. Incident AF was defined from coded diagnoses in the MIMIC-IV hospital records linked to MIMIC-IV-ECG. At one year, BTTN with the clinical term had the highest area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) of the methods compared, 0.7776 (95\% confidence interval [0.7664, 0.7888]). The clinical model reached 0.7597 [0.7488, 0.7713] and the CNN 0.7350 [0.7226, 0.7469]. The ECG term alone reached 0.7397 [0.7275, 0.7517], comparable to the CNN. Fitted once as a survival model, BTTN with the clinical term had AUROCs of 0.723 to 0.745 at horizons from 120 days to 5 years after the ECG, with a 30-day blanking window. In the survival sample, which over-represents patients with AF, observed one-year AF rose from 0.55\% in the lowest risk decile to 11.4\% in the highest. Per unit of signal energy, beat-aligned occlusion changed the ECG term's risk most in the PR window. External validation outside this single health system is the next requirement.
\end{abstract}

